{
  "id": "grammar.a1.01.04.origin",
  "title": "Ser for origin",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Saying where you are from",
      "content": "Use soy de + place to say where you are from. The question ¿De dónde eres? asks where another person is from. Eres is the tú form of ser."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Origin",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Soy de Hungría.",
          "I am from Hungary."
        ],
        [
          "Soy de Vietnam.",
          "I am from Vietnam."
        ],
        [
          "¿De dónde eres?",
          "Where are you from?"
        ],
        [
          "Soy de Budapest.",
          "I am from Budapest."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "¿De dónde eres?",
          "english": "Where are you from?"
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Soy de Hungría.",
          "english": "I am from Hungary."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Soy de Hanoi.",
          "english": "I am from Hanoi."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "De means \"from/of\". In soy de + place, it marks origin. Do not translate de literally in every context; here it is part of the pattern for saying where you are from."
    }
  ]
}
